emia. Patients are often advised to make dietary adjustments to help control triglyceride levels, particularly those on parenteral nutrition. This relationship highlights the importance of patient education and adherence to dietary recommendations to prevent complications. [Data: Entities (6173); Relationships (8861)]\n\n## Impact of associated genetic disorders\n\nSeveral genetic disorders, such as deficits in apo C-II and lipoprotein lipase, contribute to the development of hipertrigliceridemia. These conditions can lead to the accumulation of lipoproteins in the blood, exacerbating the risk of elevated triglyceride levels. Understanding these associations is vital for developing targeted treatment strategies for affected individuals. [Data: Entities (6161, 6162, 6163, 6164, 6165); Relationships (8851, 8852, 8855)]\n\n## Lomitapide as a treatment option\n\nLomitapide is a medication prescribed for severe cases of hipertrigliceridemia, indicating the need for pharmacological intervention in managing this condition. The availability of such treatments highlights the importance of comprehensive care for patients with elevated triglyceride levels, particularly those who do not respond adequately to dietary management alone. [Data: Entities (6171); Relationships (8859)]", "# Pancreatitis Lipémica and Fenofibrate Community\n\nThis community focuses on the relationship between pancreatitis lipémica, a serious health condition linked to elevated triglyceride levels, and fenofibrate, a medication used to manage these triglyceride levels. The entities are interconnected through their roles in the management and implications of hypertriglyceridemia.\n\n## Pancreatitis Lipémica as a serious health condition\n\nPancreatitis lipémica is a significant medical condition characterized by inflammation of the pancreas, primarily caused by high levels of triglycerides in the blood. This condition can lead to severe health complications, including acute pancreatitis, which may require